  "summary": "Elaborately painted Jewish marriage contracts framing legal text with birds, zodiacs and architectural borders.",
  "description": "The ketubah records a Jewish bridegroom's obligations in Aramaic legalese — and in many communities that document became a canvas. Italian Renaissance and Baroque ketubot from Venice, Florence and Ancona enclose the text in trompe-l'oeil architecture, putti, birds and zodiac medallions; Sephardi and Mizrahi traditions favor papercut lacework and floral micro-architecture. Because the contract must remain legible, illumination always frames rather than fills, making law and ornament co-guarantors of the marriage.",
  "culturalContext": "According to the Babylonian Talmud, the ketubah was enacted by Simeon ben Shetach so that it might not be a light thing for a man to divorce his wife. The enactment provides for a man's wife to receive a fixed sum of money, usually accruing from his property, in the event of his divorcing her or of his predeceasing her. Sefer ha-Chinuch suggests a different reason: \"...the Torah has commanded us to perform an act before taking a wife, a matter that is intended to show that they are a couple united in wedlock before he lies down with her carnally, and that he not come upon her as one would do to a harlot, where there is no other act that precedes what goes on between them...\"",
